  {
   "type": "events",
   "id": "584136",
   "attributes": {
    "id": 584136,
    "title": "University of Perugia microwave talk series - Antenna Systems for Space Applications at NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory",
    "description": "<p data-path-to-node=\"4\"><span style=\"font-size: 18pt;\"><strong>Microwave talk series at the University of Perugia</strong></span></p>\r\n<p data-path-to-node=\"4\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p style=\"language: it; line-height: normal; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 8.0pt; margin-left: 0in; text-align: center; direction: ltr; unicode-bidi: embed; mso-line-break-override: none; word-break: normal; punctuation-wrap: hanging;\"><span style=\"font-size: 27.0pt; font-family: Aptos; mso-ascii-font-family: Aptos; mso-fareast-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-font-family: Arial; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; color: black; mso-color-index: 1; mso-font-kerning: 12.0pt; language: en-US; font-weight: bold; mso-style-textfill-type: solid; mso-style-textfill-fill-themecolor: text1; mso-style-textfill-fill-color: black; mso-style-textfill-fill-alpha: 100.0%;\">Antenna Systems for Space Applications at NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory</span><span style=\"font-size: 27.0pt; font-family: Aptos; mso-ascii-font-family: Aptos; mso-fareast-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-font-family: Arial; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; color: black; mso-color-index: 1; mso-font-kerning: 12.0pt; language: en-US; mso-style-textfill-type: solid; mso-style-textfill-fill-themecolor: text1; mso-style-textfill-fill-color: black; mso-style-textfill-fill-alpha: 100.0%;\">&nbsp;</span></p>\r\n<p style=\"language: it; line-height: normal; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 8.0pt; margin-left: 0in; text-align: center; direction: ltr; unicode-bidi: embed; mso-line-break-override: none; word-break: normal; punctuation-wrap: hanging;\"><span style=\"font-size: 24pt; font-family: Aptos; color: rgb(224, 62, 45); font-weight: bold;\">Dr. Paolo Focardi</span></p>\r\n<p style=\"language: it; line-height: normal; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 8.0pt; margin-left: 0in; text-align: center; direction: ltr; unicode-bidi: embed; mso-line-break-override: none; word-break: normal; punctuation-wrap: hanging;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18pt; font-family: Aptos; color: black;\">Group Lead for Technology Development and New Business</span></p>\r\n<p style=\"language: it; line-height: normal; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 8.0pt; margin-left: 0in; text-align: center; direction: ltr; unicode-bidi: embed; mso-line-break-override: none; word-break: normal; punctuation-wrap: hanging;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18pt; font-family: Aptos; color: black;\">Jet Propulsion Laboratory, California Institute of Technology </span></p>\r\n<p data-path-to-node=\"4\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14.0pt; font-family: Aptos; mso-ascii-font-family: Aptos; mso-fareast-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-font-family: Arial; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; color: black; mso-color-index: 1; mso-font-kerning: 12.0pt; language: en-US; mso-style-textfill-type: solid; mso-style-textfill-fill-themecolor: text1; mso-style-textfill-fill-color: black; mso-style-textfill-fill-alpha: 100.0%;\">Antenna developments for space applications have a long history at NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory. Starting from the 70&rsquo;s, Voyager, Galileo, Cassini, SRTM and more recently Aquarius, SMAP, SWOT, NISAR and INCUS are just a few major flight projects that required the design and development of very complex antenna systems for telecom, radar and radiometric applications. They were, and in most cases still are, the state of the art in antenna design for space applications. In the past we mostly relied on relatively simple RF models and formulations and did a considerable amount of prototype testing to validate antenna performance. In recent years RF modeling tools like HFSS</span><span style=\"font-size: 14.0pt; font-family: Aptos; mso-ascii-font-family: Aptos; mso-fareast-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-font-family: Arial; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; color: black; mso-color-index: 1; mso-font-kerning: 12.0pt; language: en-US; vertical-align: super; mso-text-raise: 30%; mso-style-textfill-type: solid; mso-style-textfill-fill-themecolor: text1; mso-style-textfill-fill-color: black; mso-style-textfill-fill-alpha: 100.0%;\">TM</span><span style=\"font-size: 14.0pt; font-family: Aptos; mso-ascii-font-family: Aptos; mso-fareast-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-font-family: Arial; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; color: black; mso-color-index: 1; mso-font-kerning: 12.0pt; language: en-US; mso-style-textfill-type: solid; mso-style-textfill-fill-themecolor: text1; mso-style-textfill-fill-color: black; mso-style-textfill-fill-alpha: 100.0%;\">, GRASP</span><span style=\"font-size: 14.0pt; font-family: Aptos; mso-ascii-font-family: Aptos; mso-fareast-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-font-family: Arial; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; color: black; mso-color-index: 1; mso-font-kerning: 12.0pt; language: en-US; vertical-align: super; mso-text-raise: 30%; mso-style-textfill-type: solid; mso-style-textfill-fill-themecolor: text1; mso-style-textfill-fill-color: black; mso-style-textfill-fill-alpha: 100.0%;\">TM</span><span style=\"font-size: 14.0pt; font-family: Aptos; mso-ascii-font-family: Aptos; mso-fareast-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-font-family: Arial; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; color: black; mso-color-index: 1; mso-font-kerning: 12.0pt; language: en-US; mso-style-textfill-type: solid; mso-style-textfill-fill-themecolor: text1; mso-style-textfill-fill-color: black; mso-style-textfill-fill-alpha: 100.0%;\"> and a few more, have reached capabilities that were unimaginable just a few years back. At the same time, computer power and available memory have been increasing steadily. Thanks to this fertile ground it&rsquo;s now possible to run extremely complex RF simulations of very large structures in a relatively short time with very good results. This presentation will guide the audience through a few examples of flight projects where very complex RF models have provided extremely accurate results and allowed the development of very complex instruments, some of which are still flying today. </span></span></p>\r\n<p data-path-to-node=\"4\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p data-path-to-node=\"4\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Organizers:</span></p>\r\n<p data-path-to-node=\"4\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p data-path-to-node=\"4\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>Prof. Cristiano Tomassoni&nbsp;</strong></span></p>\r\n<p data-path-to-node=\"4\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Chair, IEEE Italy Section Joint Chapter AP03/MTT17 C&amp;S</span></span></p>\r\n<p data-path-to-node=\"4\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Univ. Of Perugia</span></span></p>\r\n<p data-path-to-node=\"4\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p data-path-to-node=\"4\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong><span lang=\"EN-US\">Prof. Roberto Vincenti Gatti</span></strong></span></p>\r\n<p data-path-to-node=\"4\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Univ. of Perugia</span></span></p>",
    "start-time": "2026-11-03T14:00:00.000Z",
    "end-time": "2026-11-03T16:00:00.000Z",
    "time-zone": {
     "name": "Europe/Rome",
     "utc-offset": 1
